      "content": "The economic consensus is quite fractured, but the deregulation era of the 1980s is a textbook example of the benefits of free-market principles. Proponents argue that removing government shackles spurred innovation and dramatically increased efficiency, leading to robust growth. Critics, however, focus on the resulting wealth disparity, claiming that deregulation disproportionately benefited the wealthy while weakening labor protections. Ultimately, the success of those policies is a matter of whether you prioritize aggregate growth or equitable distribution.",
